          "question": "I ate shellfish ten minutes ago and now my lips are swollen and my throat feels tight. Should I use my adrenaline pen?",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now.** Your symptoms match the warning signs of anaphylaxis, a severe allergic reaction that can close the airway. Swollen lips and a tight throat after eating shellfish are the kind of signs the guidance says to treat as an emergency right away.\n\nThe guidance I'm working from (the \"Anaphylaxis and severe allergic reaction\" summary, educational) says that if an adrenaline auto-injector is available, you should use it immediately into the outer thigh, following the device's own instructions, before or while calling. So yes, if you have a pen, use it now and call emergency services at the same time. Do not wait to see whether the symptoms settle. If someone else is with you, ask them to call while you do this.\n\nWhile you wait, the breathing guidance (the \"Severe difficulty breathing\" summary) suggests sitting upright, loosening tight clothing, and staying as calm as you can. Do not drive yourself to hospital.\n\nThis is general educational information, not a diagnosis, and I can't tell you about dosing. Your emergency services or clinician can guide you further.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
